#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084102} :activity/id #uuid "ec6c85e9-6830-57b2-8009-2f8599700364" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048135 :topic/id #uuid "8257a26c-85bf-580e-9d81-e2194473d981" :topic/math-academy-id 793} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084442 :tutorial/content "Suppose we roll a fair die. Then, the sample space $\\mathcal S$ of this experiment is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{S} = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the event\n$X = \\{6\\}$.\nThis means that $X$ is the event \"the die lands on $6$.\" We know that\n\n$$\nP(X) = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nbecause there are $6$ equally likely outcomes and only $1$ of those outcomes is a $6$.\n\nWe can represent the entire sample space $\\mathcal S$, the event $X$, and its probability using a **Venn diagram**.\n\n![](images/96/966be6be009751b755fbcaca9f037e0c4ee281525a94e50a9a19631dc7cd4e1f.png)\n\nNote the following important points:\n\n- The area inside the rectangle represents the sample space $\\mathcal S$.\n- The area inside the circle (shaded blue) represents the event $X$.\n- The number inside the blue circle represents the probability of $X$.\n- The area inside the rectangle but outside the circle represents the event $X'$, the complement of $X$. In our case, the complement is\n$X' = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$,\nwhich consists of every event other than throwing a $6$.\n\nFor any event $X$, we have\n\n$$\nP(X) +P\\left(X'\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nNote that the above formula can be rearranged into either of the following equivalent forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X) &= 1 - P(X^{′}) \\\\\nP(X^{′}) &= 1 - P(X)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can calculate\n\n$$\nP\\left(X'\\right)\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X^{′}) &= 1 - P(X) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample space $\\mathcal S$ contains every possible event, we sometimes write\n\n$$\nP(\\mathcal S) = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "af5371a2-dcfe-5de0-b0ab-f82c96a0b759" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3657 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93f32069-023f-5d69-983e-2273e36c3952" :step/math-academy-id 20492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084132}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083011 :question/id #uuid "c9db939d-eb4f-5516-b95d-666e235a95c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5621"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbb5348e-e292-513f-8b0a-90428692fc0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049752}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Probability of the Complement Given the Probability of the Event"} :step/id #uuid "87f1844e-77bf-5d6f-a5cc-5bf66a479b5f" :step/math-academy-id 20495 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083213 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083012 :question/id #uuid "fc8c6d6b-1596-5732-afe0-783b176339be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5624"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af4cfa4a-7f49-50c9-a875-ce51c3874c4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516821}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Probability of an Event Given the Probability of the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "113d7621-4637-5ddb-bcfe-a8f99fc1bb72" :step/math-academy-id 20502 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083214 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083014 :question/id #uuid "a761cb7f-122c-5ca0-8e90-109e0df60f3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5630"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01a9cd80-086d-5667-b4ad-296728e9ec09" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050994}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083695}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Venn Diagram for a Given Sample Space"} :step/id #uuid "ab03ce26-02b2-5bee-a351-687d9a193e8a" :step/math-academy-id 20504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084132 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083030 :question/id #uuid "a8f4aa82-b372-5569-804e-ecb8c8ebad4f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "450401ad-3c58-5c15-ab78-b5e1ccf42a85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234453}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Venn Diagram is Correct"} :step/id #uuid "c9efaccc-7297-5304-9366-ffdbc569b338" :step/math-academy-id 20705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084103}}] :activity/title "Venn Diagrams in Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083470}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516962} :activity/id #uuid "e2fcbe2f-4ff6-58b3-8208-814e82a85124" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048135 :topic/id #uuid "8257a26c-85bf-580e-9d81-e2194473d981" :topic/math-academy-id 793} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64e91312-ff58-54f3-b439-a8ba0e101cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516332} {:answer/id #uuid "a2568178-6c14-5310-b06c-f6da6cadd369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516446} {:answer/id #uuid "a89f9d8f-ba4e-55a2-9762-c20470143c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516463} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0bbe30-3e25-5367-88f5-88e0431bffb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516541} {:answer/id #uuid "d990847b-1002-5b97-9e33-5b92b5b1594f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516569}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516463} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3be114-0077-5092-b1f3-6cfed4a013eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8518694b-ea1e-5380-a35c-13f3072f6df4" :question/math-academy-id "q-84440" :question/problem "![](images/52/5245725446c23cc37e5ca4ad5b4def939c1544100eb3488092f59c4ed0f8beaa.png)\n\nConsider the sample space $S$ with the Venn diagram given above. What is $P(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given Venn diagram, we have $P(A)=y$ and $P({A}^{′})=\\frac{y}{22}.$\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A)+P({A}^{′}) & =1 \\\\ y+\\frac{y}{22} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{23y}{22} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{22}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,  $P(A)=y=\\frac{22}{23}.$"} :step/id #uuid "20e5a0d7-e4a4-5527-9522-5eac4387dd43"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "618e6135-f56b-58e3-9328-1dabdd58e75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516325} {:answer/id #uuid "98e412be-1a3f-5243-b5d3-e6079198e12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516428} {:answer/id #uuid "bc95322b-4d62-5251-8e5a-af2d36102789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.551" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516508} {:answer/id #uuid "c03ce4f2-8551-5443-840b-a6936ad84d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516521} {:answer/id #uuid "d82264de-339c-5e57-9840-e930e1482ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.521" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516325} :answer-field/id #uuid "dee1b406-0379-5d58-ba7e-b9ef815df898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7764a147-f3f7-5293-8ab2-21a4f6d2919a" :question/math-academy-id "q-84435" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a347f23ec8c99fda74de190515eb7aa2d071be29ca106b99116b3d050cb8a1fe.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P(X).$" :question/worked-solution "The Venn diagram tells us that $P({X}^{′})=0.749.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X) & =1-P({X}^{′}) \\\\  & =1-0.749 \\\\  & =0.251.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2c9093fe-c1c5-5ee0-9d9c-ed832e4df4f4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "845355a0-843b-51f7-8c0d-52009b5363fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079812} {:answer/id #uuid "93f57bbd-68cb-5590-b469-f8dec98c1443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079813} {:answer/id #uuid "b495be06-7373-56b0-9303-363912083a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079814} {:answer/id #uuid "f552a0c7-fea4-5002-ae66-4c9943b9551b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079815} {:answer/id #uuid "26fabe08-9668-5bfd-b855-ea3411ba9fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079812} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ccf4c3-ab1b-50f2-bb00-0b00faa94fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186079811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "991f3941-ac00-506d-94c9-cb69a7d61849" :question/math-academy-id "q-11322" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c092436e6f2f66cd2ade33818209f001d1481f962c086da09c284975af83795.png)\n\nGiven the above sample space, find $P({X}^{′}).$" :question/worked-solution "The Venn diagram tells us that $P(X)=\\frac{2}{5}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({X}^{′}) & =1-P(X) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c6e45cc7-205e-57d1-80c2-e67d0fe17e04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516885}}] :activity/title "Venn Diagrams in Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516110}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069736} :activity/id #uuid "7a01410a-a015-53d5-ab5a-e3aa1ddf8e50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048345 :topic/id #uuid "9779346a-0ae5-5b28-ae74-23e99f006542" :topic/math-academy-id 426} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069932 :tutorial/content "We can analyze the world around us by **modeling** situations using equations. Modeling means to write down an equation that fits the scenario, and then solve it to find some quantity that is of interest. We can use linear equations to model specific situations.\n\nTo solve a **modeling problem**, we should:\n\n1. Read the problem from start to finish.\n2. Look for a question within the problem (usually at the end).\n3. Start by writing, \"Let $x=$ something.\" Usually, $x$ is some **unknown quantity** that the question is asking for.\n4. Reread the question, and try to write down an equation for $x$ that must be true.\n5. Solve the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a3da58c-57e8-5853-a827-179e28b62176" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1408 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "edd80f99-4dd0-5450-a69d-ee9019f1c5a7" :step/math-academy-id 3067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068809 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068662 :question/id #uuid "62bd5b25-6b48-5e76-b338-cd4491d9341b" :question/math-academy-id "e-916"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d806fdb4-05d2-5b37-afe9-78a9c2b53ac1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048925}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235516}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Using Linear Equations With One Variable Term"} :step/id #uuid "49140671-fe24-58ea-86a4-9f8a268818b1" :step/math-academy-id 3068 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068663 :question/id #uuid "7f14ed31-f9b6-554a-8dff-34fa5d646335" :question/math-academy-id "e-918"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30abea2b-3b0e-5d3f-88c3-deb7c9ada4c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512892}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Using Linear Equations With Two Variable Terms"} :step/id #uuid "98e328dd-3ea2-5d5d-a079-3665cda7badc" :step/math-academy-id 3073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068668 :question/id #uuid "f989a0c3-225e-52d6-bb79-1cdfe79746c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9db4b54-14b4-55be-8d05-0a7a7e33315e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069265}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Using Equations Containing One Fractional Term"} :step/id #uuid "921765b1-cc81-5756-91ac-976dcdd6472d" :step/math-academy-id 36027 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069738}}] :activity/title "Modeling With Linear Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068959}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055747} :activity/id #uuid "bfe70dfc-da4d-55b4-a070-178f1424a5d4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048470 :topic/id #uuid "a249df7c-110c-5944-a303-74505130a629" :topic/math-academy-id 28} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055970 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to evaluate $\\log_{4}9$, but our calculator only has a key for the common logarithm $\\log$ (base $10$).\n\nIn such cases, we can use the **change of base formula**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIt effectively allows us to change a logarithm from one base, $a$, to another, $b$. Note that $X$, $a$, and $b$ have to be all greater than zero and that\n\n$$\na \\ne 1\n$$\n\n$$\nb \\ne 1\n$$\n\nUsing this formula, we can rewrite the logarithm from base $a=4$ to base $b=10$, to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{4} 9 &= \\frac{\\log 9}{\\log 4} \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{0.954242}{0.602059} \\\\\n&= 1.585\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "bb104b87-e850-5cff-9826-fdd61c0287d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "59ad328c-2f78-5aea-b0d1-6ed4bc8f3800" :step/math-academy-id 467832 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054541 :question/id #uuid "15844e53-9573-5e6f-8fef-debbe0a142ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-4091"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87ee4eae-702f-59f0-aff4-46c9acb89f6c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234223}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Logarithm Using the Change of Base Formula"} :step/id #uuid "eb11f291-8fc6-5293-8cde-d3a6827123ce" :step/math-academy-id 467833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054543 :question/id #uuid "890afebc-d130-5dbc-aaf7-c2cd4db193f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4100"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bccd26ff-1dc5-5817-962b-a1ef1f46b2fb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234501}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm With a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1afbdf86-d560-5101-b414-37b5d5f9909a" :step/math-academy-id 467837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054540 :question/id #uuid "fc85eeb4-dbc0-5ea7-af3a-60270d6ae722" :question/math-academy-id "e-4089"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ab12b8-b1c9-5192-91be-cebc968d9587" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234488}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Logarithm Without a Calculator Using the Change of Base Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1a1e953e-dcb3-521f-91dc-5eadcdc1b57e" :step/math-academy-id 467840}] :activity/title "The Change of Base Formula for Logarithms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054890}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074923} :activity/id #uuid "a8efd125-dcb7-576a-aba7-d6a258e9a53d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048583 :topic/id #uuid "ab9e96eb-9d23-50fe-8a94-f2eebf047bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 491} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073656 :question/id #uuid "f89ebcc9-96b7-5d6b-b844-4a9328f2ab6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7916"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87cdc7a8-9437-56cc-a523-6b869b013428" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050659}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516862}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Transformed Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "2480a381-e1e9-5ddf-97c9-5065c97a8cda" :step/math-academy-id 29494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074984 :tutorial/content "Any transformation that maps a polygon to a similar polygon is called a **similarity transformation**.\n\nAny combination of transformations made up of translation, rotation, reflection, and dilation will result in a similarity transformation.\n\nTo understand why this is true, remember:\n\n- Rigid motions (rotation, reflection, and translation) do not change the lengths or angles of the original figure. They map polygons to congruent polygons. If two polygons are congruent, then they are similar.\n- A dilation with scale factor $k$ multiplies all the lengths of the original shape by $k$ (leaving the angle measures unchanged), thereby producing a similar shape." :tutorial/id #uuid "622763c1-840b-5946-a1e7-feb59d8f0c89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2315 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7ec1bc50-2134-5ed0-aa3d-e29348fd99e7" :step/math-academy-id 9647 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073602 :question/id #uuid "54f05d2d-1137-5bc7-b56d-0215dc3895ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-2978"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7545aba-d80b-58ab-9a56-25ad196da3ee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074184}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Similarity Transformation Given a Polygon and Its Image"} :step/id #uuid "b8d1a4d2-47c1-5676-a5b1-d16b02e3ee67" :step/math-academy-id 9655 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073603 :question/id #uuid "a75b57a8-4819-594a-9aa2-e35b54a68f6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2993"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8b5ef8e-6f13-5496-ae7f-c9fbaf3e25c7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516855}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Similarity Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "3698100e-df7d-5666-8520-aa605b0f8eca" :step/math-academy-id 9670}] :activity/title "Similarity Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073926}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516979} :activity/id #uuid "de7cd668-3db3-5af6-b281-2d79561e3e20" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048583 :topic/id #uuid "ab9e96eb-9d23-50fe-8a94-f2eebf047bbc" :topic/math-academy-id 491} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed52675-1674-5013-b977-2c1156e5c97e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516169} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6eb254-4764-5546-881b-d06219358af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516576} {:answer/id #uuid "de8ea9cd-d289-5e31-b480-860682054345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516581} {:answer/id #uuid "def95e8e-df89-5823-b269-5d8d58bb3e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516584} {:answer/id #uuid "eb50b3d4-f784-52f7-8f87-e4315c20fe44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516576} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5beb74-044c-5e40-8586-8ba7b7f03022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8890925-6102-5a39-922f-cbb90bd120c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-89714" :question/problem "A square $ABCD$ is translated by $3$ units to the left and $3$ units upward. If the resulting quadrilateral is $PQRS,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $ABCD∼PQRS$\n\nII. $ABCD≅PQRS$\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{BC}≅\\overset{―}{QR}$" :question/worked-solution "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations. \n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We are given a combination of translations. As a result, we obtain that \n\n$$\nABCD∼PQRS.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since our combined transformation consists of rigid motions only (namely, translations), the distance between all points is preserved under the transformation. Therefore,  we obtain that \n\n$$\nABCD≅PQRS.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $ABCD≅PQRS,$ all corresponding sides must be congruent. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC}≅\\overset{―}{QR}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "34047e62-3d93-591d-8a51-e16282e2c37f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f1557f-aa1b-5c6b-97f9-3183a0fdae13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516243} {:answer/id #uuid "5eeba46f-9dda-59a4-86da-d1ee0173c9ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516317} {:answer/id #uuid "8861175f-b2e1-5165-afbc-bffd586e1f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516397} {:answer/id #uuid "be0af016-3822-5b80-b2b9-b035f07b9902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516511} {:answer/id #uuid "c746c42b-4136-5b7a-8e70-85095e0c0424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516511} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4eeffe0-d387-54ea-99bf-7c07fda39ad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1ea750dd-05b5-599b-a75b-1d40366d85dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-89161" :question/problem "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. Stretching with stretch factor $6$ in the $x$-direction, then a translation by $2$ units downward\n\nII. Rotation by ${45}^{∘}$ clockwise around a point $O$ followed by reflection over a line $l$\n\nIII. Dilation of scale factor $4$ with center at $A,$ then translation by $6$ units to the right" :question/worked-solution "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I includes stretching in only one direction. Thus, it is not a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II is a combination of rotation and a reflection, which are rigid motions. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III is a combination of dilation of scale factor $4$ and a translation. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ccd3e0af-ff85-5d17-8152-0c5d9758036f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4dea26-3eb3-5f68-9eb1-02c2680a892f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516131} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7e6e72-0b09-5a44-9520-dfe8cf8a7a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516350} {:answer/id #uuid "eaba3792-c563-5079-bcd1-af361f15f0d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516600} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e5c56-e5ce-55fd-bc03-e48b07508467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516638} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9917d3-0d49-551d-bb4f-aa7e5e3ff567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516131} :answer-field/id #uuid "eec1a239-a69c-51bb-a8aa-9e0e02460536" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cfe4ea96-fddc-550c-ac7a-6ec2d2c94652" :question/math-academy-id "q-53703" :question/problem "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\nI. $(x,y)↦(x-5,y)$\n\nII. $(x,y)↦(-y,x)$\n\nIII. $(x,y)↦(x,3y)$" :question/worked-solution "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations. \n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I represents a translation by $5$ units to the left. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation II represents a rotation by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise around the origin. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n\n- Transformation III represents a stretch of factor $3$ in the $y$-direction. This is not a rigid motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "eab95375-eb28-5e00-b681-d227e2c00467" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516967}}] :activity/title "Similarity Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516108}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055844} :activity/id #uuid "0e31a49b-f54b-50cd-ab36-02966e00c737" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/id #uuid "ac718bb5-4681-5ba0-883a-072801cad492" :topic/math-academy-id 35} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055520 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055962 :tutorial/content "For a function $f(x)$, we can express the derivative as $f'(x)$.\n\nRecall that we can also express the derivative using the **differential operator**, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the differential operator while computing the derivative, too. For example, the power rule can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\left(x^n \\right) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\big(\\cdots\\big)\n$$\n\njust means that we need to differentiate whatever is inside $\\big(\\cdots\\big)$ with respect to $x$." :tutorial/id #uuid "537fb074-19a0-542c-9a84-c4542e26b33f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2647 :tutorial/title "The Differential Operator"} :step/id #uuid "219dccb2-c3f8-5ef8-af33-9ea8f7e5c0f8" :step/math-academy-id 12596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055978 :tutorial/content "We know how to differentiate a function like\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\nusing the definition of the derivative. However, there is a trick to do it faster!\n\nThe trick is called the **power rule**, and it states that for a general power function in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthe derivative is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nThe function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^2\n$$\n\ntakes the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^n\n$$\n\nwith $n=2$, so we can use the power rule to compute its derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= nx^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2 - 1} \\\\\n&= 2x^{1} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result agrees with what we would get if we used the definition of the derivative. However, using the power rule, the computations are much easier!" :tutorial/id #uuid "37492dee-669b-5eb2-9676-b78afaf6acb4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 337 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ac48d370-ba29-5343-bf37-4b82f3611773" :step/math-academy-id 625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054455 :question/id #uuid "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32dd6880-3725-564a-9e85-0d39b6960c72" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055196}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "ea42d926-8045-5d22-9aa7-2fd3a3f233c8" :step/math-academy-id 626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054456 :question/id #uuid "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58" :question/math-academy-id "e-135"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2ff1808-d239-5625-93b7-21a98ada3fab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055347}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Negative Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "8893f4d9-c611-591e-80a2-799835854aae" :step/math-academy-id 629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055520}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054457 :question/id #uuid "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab" :question/math-academy-id "e-136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050894}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519521}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "41c0eff0-f259-5568-b570-bafddf204af8" :step/math-academy-id 631 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055848}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055983 :tutorial/content "In general, if\n\n$$\nf(x) = k\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant, then $f'(x) = 0$. There are a couple ways to see why this is.\n\nFirst, let's take the constant function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2\n$$\n\nbut write it a little differently:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2x^0\n$$\n\nTo differentiate the function above, we can use the power rule with $n=0$. Applying the power rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 0 \\cdot 2x^{0 - 1} \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 2x^{-1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere's nothing special about the number $2$. The same result would hold for *any* constant function.\n\nWe can also think about this geometrically. The function $y=f(x)=2$ is a horizontal line, and it does not rise or fall as we change $x$. So, its slope is zero everywhere, and therefore $f'(x) = 0$ everywhere.\n\n![](images/22/222febb3babc289ba15ba1e187aa9848ea6f2d802bf21ad8e1396b092370d564.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "aba9943d-6c1b-5f0d-b9e0-a0c854c231b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 341 :tutorial/title "The Derivative of the Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "421f20ea-c000-57af-973a-3496371dd497" :step/math-academy-id 633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055849}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054465 :question/id #uuid "27baaab1-2088-5277-be24-5dfba2bfa5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-137"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "654f8868-880b-53dc-ba4c-677aecb10b95" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048262}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519576}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Constant Function Using the Power Rule"} :step/id #uuid "814ed39b-61cd-5eb5-92ed-72ff4dbe9a1b" :step/math-academy-id 634}] :activity/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054897}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519665} :activity/id #uuid "8d6616d8-4bd9-5180-836d-7a649de8f37f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/id #uuid "ac718bb5-4681-5ba0-883a-072801cad492" :topic/math-academy-id 35} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38afd3e0-5bd1-5aff-bde9-5ab30100b8a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100{x}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519078} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb3a68d-3bc9-5a2e-aa01-8fd19f73ef1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{-100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519114} {:answer/id #uuid "982e4a51-8007-577a-887e-87a9e653b741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519242} {:answer/id #uuid "d47195f6-f6ac-5acf-9ac4-5fd2c4c5e7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519340} {:answer/id #uuid "eea7b9dd-78cc-5e09-a8b2-9e91f80d4798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519384}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519340} :answer-field/id #uuid "6151e229-0e35-564f-8d7f-5b331c2a89a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7712c3e1-7e39-5f21-96a2-73fbade1bb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-47827" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-100.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "64201a69-4087-5fa9-8613-459e7ef99140" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f3bef3-f4f9-50bd-ad1b-980bacc7743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519030} {:answer/id #uuid "1a413cde-51aa-554a-a668-cb72400d7998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519032} {:answer/id #uuid "22ad66f0-9dd6-500b-bfa1-89777d6d096e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519046} {:answer/id #uuid "93027069-aad5-57c6-9c9d-a24023cbdefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519234} {:answer/id #uuid "b42a8058-7cc4-5b42-8aee-1b65e58718bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519030} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2dfe4a5-5448-5650-8cd1-67d2d7210c41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "adc818d6-3be7-557c-93b2-e7ce26f749ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-102182" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{1}{e}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "bcda3cd1-5fdc-5593-8afc-4d6c3c5925cb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3490041-c6e1-534b-ae84-df116add6226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519262} {:answer/id #uuid "da522b4a-46a4-5729-803b-4a8ed45b4b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519352} {:answer/id #uuid "f16e8de5-d93f-5c40-b48d-326156982a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519389} {:answer/id #uuid "f927c200-f0bd-5da8-96d4-fd1b9ab95850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519406} {:answer/id #uuid "fcac43f6-9d84-52e6-bbc3-c7111b1c3113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519409} :answer-field/id #uuid "db460c81-b20d-5e16-92de-4539fa40eda4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0cf33d77-f213-525d-99fb-8b9182428af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-12244" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ when $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/2}}={x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2\\,-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d2cd4ad0-adea-528f-a78f-3a888cbb65e5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519637}}] :activity/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518981}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055752} :activity/id #uuid "9d007e60-3573-5f17-b906-19fa809ad32a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048669 :topic/id #uuid "b371486a-80cf-552b-9506-22930ee7f95d" :topic/math-academy-id 88} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056025 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the factored polynomial\n\n$$\np(x)= (x-5)^2(x+8)\n$$\n\nBy the factor theorem, we know the roots of the polynomial are $x=5$ and $x=-8$.\n\nThe **multiplicity** of a root of a polynomial equals the number of times the corresponding factor appears in the fully factored form of the polynomial.\n\nFor the polynomial $p(x)$ above, we have the following:\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. Therefore, it appears *twice*, and the multiplicity of the root $x=5$ is $2$.\n- In contrast, the factor $(x+8)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. Therefore, it appears only once, and the multiplicity of the root $x=-8$ is $1$.\n\nIf the multiplicity of a root is $1$, like $x=-8$ in the example above, then the root is called a **simple root**. Otherwise, the root is called a **multiple root**. In the example above, $x=5$ is a multiple root.\n\nAdditionally, roots that have multiplicity $2$ are often referred to as **double roots.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "65a93c16-e7a1-5275-8033-1b31d9afc792" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8125 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a130fe56-5586-51b5-9c77-4172d47d93c8" :step/math-academy-id 472295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054407 :question/id #uuid "d7dee7aa-bbb9-50ba-9b0c-6aa0fecbe49d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12994"} :knowledge-p